  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low storm chances continue today with higher humidity \nand hotter temperatures. More storms Friday as the front sags south.\n\nIt's another mostly dry day ahead with just a few spotty showers and \nt-storms across the region. Still monitoring the upper level ridge \nnearby over the central and southeastern U.S along with a nearby \nstalled front draped from the VA Tidewater westward into the TN/Ohio \nRiver Valleys. Pieces of energy will continue to traverse this \nboundary within west to northwest flow today through Friday. \nConfidence in timing and thunderstorm coverage is low for today \ngiven the heightened ridge with increasing chances Friday as the \nridge flattens and front moves through. \n\nAs for today, expect any residual showers early this morning over \nnortheast MD, southern MD, and central VA to quickly end as a weak \nupper level trough pushes east prior to 8z/4am. Additional spotty \nshowers and thunderstorms may dot the area later this afternoon with \na focus over the Alleghenies (due to terrain circulations) and \nacross central VA/Shenandoah Valley where the front resides. The \nfront will gradually lift back toward the north late tonight into \nFriday with another wave of low pressure set to push through. This \nwill lead to increased chances for showers and thunderstorms across \nthe region with a focus, especially along and south of I-66/US-50. \nThunderstorm activity will likely occur during the peak heating \nperiod with locally damaging winds, frequent lightning, and isolated \ninstances of flash flooding as the primary concerns. SPC continues \nto highlight areas along and south of US-33 in VA with a Marginal \nRisk (Level 1 out of 5) for severe weather. This aligns with machine \nlearning/ AI guidance which suggests 5 to 15 percent probs for \nsevere weather Friday afternoon and evening. A lot of this will be \ndependent upon cloud cover and any interaction from potential MCSs \ndiving across the central and lower Ohio River Valley and into far \nsouthern WV/southwest VA. Some guidance remains aggressive like the \nNAMnest while the HRRR, ARW, and NSSL remain more reasonable with \ncoverage/magnitude. No washouts are expected although chances will \nincrease during the peak heating period, especially south and west \nof the I-95 metro areas. \n\nHigh pressure builds from the north Friday night shunting the front \nfurther south of the region. This will allow for drier conditions to \nprevail along with a brief break in the humidity. Highs today will \npush into the mid 80s and low 90s with heat index values in the mid \nto upper 90s east of the Alleghenies. Overnight lows will fall back \ninto the upper 60s and low 70s with highs Friday into the 80s. \n\nAmple high pressure builds to the north of the region on Saturday \nshunting the front into the Carolinas. An onshore component to the \nwind could keep some additional cloud cover around with partly \ncloudy skies expected. Humidity will briefly drop with highs in the \nlow to mid 80s (upper 80s in the city centers and 70s over the \nmountains).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Showers and thunderstorms possible between Sunday \nand  Monday before becoming drier into early next week.\n\nBeginning Sunday, an upper-level perturbation forms over the \nCanadian Rockies and tracks eastward, with a stemming frontal \nboundary tracking across the East Coast late Sunday and into Monday. \nThis boundary will likely bring chances for scattered showers and \nthunderstorms here in the Mid-Atlantic, although model guidance has \nbeen somewhat marginal on the severe potential for this event. As of \nthe 18z ensemble runs, several outputs have the highest likelihood \nfor severe weather just south of the area towards southern VA and \nthe Carolinas, while the GFS ensemble runs still have a 15%-30% \nprobability for severe weather centered within the region. Should \nany thunderstorms become severe, the most likely hazards will likely \nbe gusty winds as well as locally heavy rainfall. As drought \nconditions continue to improve within the region, flooding potential \nbecomes more of a concern with any heavy rainfall-producing \nthunderstorms that materialize. \n\nOtherwise, temperatures remain near-normal for mid-August, with \nhumid conditions as well as temperatures ranging in the 80s and \nlower-90s at times near the metros. High pressure moves in around \nthe Ohio River Valley, shifting surface winds coming out of the NW. \nOnce that frontal boundary passes offshore by Tuesday, it could \nbring in some much needed relief to the humid conditions expected \nthis weekend.",
